WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.533
Building an MCP server.

2
00:00:02.833 --> 00:00:08.533
With a few lines of Python, your own system becomes a tool that any MCP client can use.

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:14.833
With FastMCP, a decorated Python function becomes an MCP tool.

4
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:17.400
The type hints generate the input schema.

5
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.833
And the docstring becomes the description the model reads.

6
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:23.100
Here is the stack.

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.433
At the bottom, your function, with types and a docstring.

8
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:29.533
FastMCP turns those hints into a schema.

9
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:33.567
It also handles the handshake and the JSON-RPC plumbing.

10
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.413
So any client can use it.

11
00:00:35.413 --> 00:00:39.000
Your job is to write good functions and honest docstrings.

12
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.533
Here is a real server.

13
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:44.833
The decorator and the typed parameter define the tool.

14
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:47.967
The docstring tells the model when and how to use it.

15
00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:52.200
The query uses a dollar parameter, never SQL built from strings.

16
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.167
And run serves it over stdio, ready for any client.

17
00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:58.933
A few rules keep this safe.

18
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:01.967
Start read-only, with parameterized selects.

19
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.700
Add write tools later, and only behind human checkpoints.

20
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.500
And write honest docstrings, because the model relies on them.

21
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.500
Here is the portfolio moment.

22
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.553
The Orbital Research Agent already stores data in Postgres.

23
00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:19.033
You wrap it with three read-only tools.

24
00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:22.100
Register the server in Claude Desktop's config.

25
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.960
Ask which asteroid had the closest approach this quarter.

26
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:30.457
Claude calls your server, your SQL runs, and the answer cites your rows.

27
00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:33.767
Almost nobody interviewing next to you has done this.

28
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:35.889
Let's recap.

29
00:01:35.889 --> 00:01:38.733
FastMCP turns a function into a tool.

30
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:42.300
Hints make the schema, and the docstring makes the description.

31
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.267
Start read-only, with parameterized SQL.

32
00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:49.174
Wrap a real system, and you have a standout project.

33
00:01:49.174 --> 00:01:51.533
Next up: connecting an MCP client.
